From: Claude (TrimUI port) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] pvr/pco: Fix fragment throughput on GE8300 (HSR, null descriptors, CWE, smp padding)

Measured on the TrimUI Smart Pro (GE8300 @ 696 MHz), see docs/gpu-perf-vs-stock.md.

1. Hidden surface removal: every application fragment shader got an injected
   sample-mask check (discard) and ISP feedback, so every draw was
   punch-through (or translucent with early_fragment_tests). The check is now
   left out when the multisample state is static, single-sampled, full mask,
   no A2C, no sample shading; ISP feedback / the EF store guard is only emitted
   if the shader can really demote/terminate. Alpha-to-one emulation is left
   out when alpha-to-one is statically disabled.
2. Null descriptors (needed by Zink): null UBOs point at the zero page with
   size 0 and null combined image samplers at a 1x1 zero texture with
   clamp-to-edge, so loads/samples need no per-access branch. Queries, fetches,
   shadow compares, SSBOs and storage/separate images keep the check; for
   combined image samplers it tests a marker in the sampler metadata
   (PCO_SAMPLER_META_NULL_IMAGE, was RSVD0).
3. VK_EXT_color_write_enable is no longer exposed: it was emulated by reading
   back the tile buffer in every shader (translucent pass, no HSR). Zink works
   without it.
4. pco_shrink_vecs also shrinks vecs with multi-channel sources (drops whole
   trailing sources). Removes the 12-14 zero moves that padded every sample
   instruction's data vector.

Results (old -> new): vkmark 207 -> 246; full-screen textured pass 9.0 -> 2.4 ms;
cage composite 18.7 -> 7.3 ms; RetroArch mGBA under rotated cage 29 -> 58.5 fps
(Vulkan) / 29 -> 56.5 fps (glcore via Zink).
